  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency WASH services</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam and its partner PEF recognize the challenges of sustainability in Gaza&#x2019;s volatile humanitarian context, where needs are ongoing and risks persist. This project integrates recovery and long-term development efforts into its humanitarian response, emphasizingcollaboration with actors like the Coastal Municipalities Water Utility (CMWU) and local authorities from the outset. Exit strategies are embedded during the proposal stage to account for risks and ensure smooth closure while promoting sustainability.

Oxfam and PEF have defined actions to enable a safe and sustainable project exit, emphasizing local ownership and long-term service continuity. CMWU&#x2019;s involvement in the project&#x2019;s design and implementation enhances institutional ownership and guarantees post-project maintenance and service delivery. Quality assurance of infrastructure, such as the water and sewer networks, will maximize lifespan and prevent premature depletion. These networks will be operated and maintained by CMWU, which has the expertise and capacity, ensuring economic sustainability.

Social sustainability is promoted through inclusive community engagement, with a focus on women, people with disabilities (PWDs), and vulnerable groups. Capacity-building and awareness sessions foster ownership among beneficiaries, ensuring continued proper use and maintenance of facilities. Oxfam also commits to transparency by sharing project plans, timelines, and exitstrategies with communities, building trust and encouraging accountability.</narrative>
  </description>
